gency relating to \"California State Motor Vehicle and Engine Pollution Control Standards; Heavy-Duty Vehicle and Engine Emission Warranty and Maintenance Provisions; Advanced Clean Trucks; Zero Emission Airport Shuttle; Zero-Emission Power Train Certification; Waiver of Preemption; Notice of Decision\".","introducedDate":"2025-04-02","latestActionDate":"2025-06-12","latestActionText":"Became Public Law No: 119-15.","policyArea":"Environmental Protection","subjects":["Air quality","California","Climate change and greenhouse gases","Federal preemption","Legislative rules and procedure","Motor carriers","Motor vehicles"],"sponsorBioguideId":"J000307","cosponsorCount":24,"committeeNames":[],"congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-joint-resolution/87","industries":[{"industry":"transport","confidence":0.95,"rationale":"The bill directly affects regulatory obligations for trucking companies and heavy-duty vehicle operators subject to California emission standards."},{"industry":"manufacturing","confidence":0.9,"rationale":"The bill would change compliance requirements for manufacturers of heavy-duty trucks and engines sold in California."},{"industry":"energy-renewable","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Revoking zero-emission truck mandates would reduce market requirements for electric vehicle charging infrastructure and clean powertrain technology."},{"industry":"utilities-electric","confidence":0.5,"rationale":"Changes to zero-emission vehicle requirements could affect electricity demand and infrastructure planning for fleet electrification."}],"plainSummary":"This resolution would cancel waivers that allow California to enforce its own stricter emission standards for heavy-duty trucks and engines. Under the Clean Air Act, California can request permission to set its own vehicle pollution rules that are tougher than federal standards; this resolution would revoke several such waivers related to truck emissions, warranties, zero-emission air